.Dd October 30, 2026
.Dt BACKLIGHT 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm backlight
.Nd the panel's backlight
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /sys/class/backlight/NAME/brightness
.Pp
.Pa /sys/class/backlight/NAME/bl_power
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A backlight lights a laptop's panel, and its level sets how bright the
screen is.
The kernel gives each backlight a driver found at boot as a directory of
.Pa /sys/class/backlight ,
named by the driver, as Linux does, so that a program written for Linux's
backlight class works unchanged:
.Bl -tag -width actual_brightness
.It Pa max_brightness
The highest level.
.It Pa brightness
The level set, from 0 to
.Pa max_brightness ;
root sets it with a write of a decimal number.
0 is as dark as the hardware goes, which may be dark enough to read
nothing on the screen.
.It Pa actual_brightness
The level the hardware has: the level set, or, while the backlight is off,
0 on a backlight that has no switch of its own.
.It Pa bl_power
0 while root leaves the backlight on and 4 once root turns it off, as
Linux's
.Dv FB_BLANK_UNBLANK
and
.Dv FB_BLANK_POWERDOWN ;
root writes either.
.It Pa type
.Ql raw :
the levels are the hardware's own, not a scale of perceived brightness.
.El
.Pp
The backlight is also off while the console is blank, whatever
.Pa bl_power
says, and comes back at the level set when the console is shown again;
see
.Xr console 4 .
The level is not kept across a reboot by the kernel; the system's init
saves it at shutdown and writes it back at boot.
.Ss intel_backlight
On a laptop whose Intel graphics and chipset are of the Haswell and
Broadwell generation, the chipset a Lynx Point or Wildcat Point, the
kernel drives the chipset's pulse-width modulator, which lights the panel,
as Linux's i915 driver does, from boot.
The backlight is
.Pa intel_backlight ,
and its levels are the modulator's period, the duty cycle being the level.
The kernel takes the modulator only when the graphics device at 0:2.0 is
Intel's and the firmware left the modulator on with a period, as it does
to light its own picture; on another machine nothing is written.
A modulator that the processor still drives in place of the chipset is
handed to the chipset's at the processor's level first.
When the firmware left the panel lit through the chipset's panel power
sequencer, the backlight is turned off and on by the sequencer's backlight
enable, as Linux's
.Pa bl_power
does, and keeps its level while it is off; otherwise it is off at a level
of 0.
On the Lenovo G50-70 the levels run from 0 to 937.
.Pp
When the
.Xr i915 4
driver binds the graphics device it takes
.Pa intel_backlight
over: the directory and
.Pa bl_power
stay, and the driver then turns the backlight off while the panel's pipe
is off and on again with it, as Linux's i915 does.
As Linux's i915, it maps the levels 1 to the period onto the modulator's
duty cycles from the least the firmware's video table allows to the
period, 18 to 937 on the Lenovo, turns the backlight off at level 0, and
gives an
.Pa actual_brightness
of 0 while the pipe is off.
When the device goes the boot's driver takes the backlight back.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /sys/class/backlight/NAME
.It Pa /sys/class/backlight/NAME
The backlight's attributes; see
.Xr sysfs 5 .
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The boot log names the backlight, its levels and how it is turned off:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
backlight: intel_backlight: lynxpoint-lp PWM, levels 0 to 937, at 937, off by the panel's enable
.Ed
.Pp
with
.Ql off by a level of 0
in place of the last part for a backlight with no switch, or, on an Intel machine whose modulator the kernel does not take, why:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
backlight: intel: none: the firmware left the PWM off
.Ed
.Pp
and, once
.Xr i915 4
takes it over:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
i915: backlight: intel_backlight: levels 0 to 937, at 937, off with the pipe
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A write of
.Pa brightness
that is not a level from 0 to
.Pa max_brightness ,
or of
.Pa bl_power
that is not 0 or 4.
.It Bq Er EACCES
A write by a user other than root.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr console 4 ,
.Xr i915 4 ,
.Xr sysfs 5
